From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] dcomp: Return a device that exists.

DCompositionCreateDevice returned E_NOTIMPL and never wrote *device, so a
caller that does not check the HRESULT reads through whatever the
variable held. GTA IV's loader does exactly that:

    fixme:dcomp:DCompositionCreateDevice 00000000, {c37ea93a-...}, 002541D0.
    err:wow:raise_guest_exception guest exception 0xc0000005 at eip 0x79f9d308
                                  esp 0x21fe64 address 0x2c

The 0x2c is a field read off the NULL it was left with. That crash had
been read as a DRM check for most of a day; it is DirectComposition being
a stub, and it is the last thing between this port and a rendered frame.
By the time it is reached DXVK has compiled its shaders through ACO and
built its pipelines and descriptor layouts.

Every method still answers E_NOTIMPL. What changes is that there is an
object to answer: a reference count and a working QueryInterface, so a
caller that ignores the HRESULT gets a pointer rather than a fault. This
is not DirectComposition and does not pretend to be -- it is the
difference between failing and crashing.
diff --git a/dlls/dcomp/device.c b/dlls/dcomp/device.c
index 2744d758e9..f7bde192f7 100644
--- a/dlls/dcomp/device.c
+++ b/dlls/dcomp/device.c
@@ -19,19 +19,268 @@
 
